tweets.text:
Christophe Barraud's Morning Brief always has a lot on Europe, and Friday it included some good news and some not so good news for incoming ECB President Pablo Hernández de Cos (following up on my post Thursday evening).

The good news is on inflation. November prices in France and Spain slowed much more than expected, per Bloomberg:
▪️ France: 3.2% year over year, down from 3.7% in October and well below the 3.6% expected;
▪️ Spain: 4.1%, down from 4.9% and below the 4.7% expected;
▪️ Energy did most of the work, but French services inflation also eased, to 2.3% from 2.6%.

Euro-area inflation for November is out Tuesday, with analysts looking for 3.9% from 4.0%. Bantleon's Daniel Hartmann sees 3.8% based on the France and Spain numbers. Traders pared their bets on more rate hikes, cutting the odds of three more by the end of 2027 to 25% from 35%, per Bloomberg.

The bad news is on growth, and France is the problem:
▪️ French GDP shrank 0.2% in the third quarter, revised from a 0.1% decline, per Insee. Output has now fallen in two of the past three quarters and was flat in the other;
▪️ French consumer spending was flat in October after a 0.9% drop in September (revised from 0.5%), leaving it down 1.2% from a year ago;
▪️ Insee says the French economy will grow just 0.1% this year if the fourth quarter is flat, and ING sees only 0.4% growth in 2027;
▪️ Spanish retail sales also fell 0.9% from a year ago in October.

Then there's the budget. French Finance Minister Roland Lescure said the weak data mean the government has to deliver a budget that cuts the deficit to 5% of GDP in 2027 from 5.4% this year, despite opposition in parliament. Not surprisingly, S&P Global Ratings on Friday evening revised France's credit outlook to "negative" from "stable," citing high budget deficits, weak growth, and political division. That comes after Fitch put it on negative outlook last month. 

At the EU level, leaders dug in on budget disputes, and France and Germany are pushing for a deal by year-end to "Le Pen-proof" the next EU budget. 

The ECB isn't convinced inflation is beaten, though. Minutes of the October meeting showed officials saw the inflation outlook worsening and further tightening as likely necessary. The ECB's consumer survey showed expected inflation over the next 12 months jumping to 3.5% in October from 3.1%.

But the mix is shifting. A month ago the ECB's problem was mostly inflation. Now inflation is cooling faster than forecast while France shrinks, the French-German 10-year spread sits near euro-crisis highs (it averaged 147 basis points in October, the widest since November 2011), and Paris has to cut its deficit into a contracting economy. 

That leaves the door wide open for de Cos to take policy in either direction after an almost certain December hike.

